h injuries.", "PACIENTE QUEMADO:The \"PACIENTE QUEMADO\" refers to a burn patient who is suffering from severe burns and requires specific nutritional assessment and support. Due to the effects of burns on metabolism and nutrition, this patient necessitates specialized nutritional support and fluid therapy to aid in their recovery.", "MUSCULATURA ESQUELÉTICA:Skeletal muscle is affected in burn patients, leading to significant nutritional challenges and risks of malnutrition.", "DEPLECIÓN INTRACELULAR:Intracellular depletion of amino acids occurs in burn patients, affecting their metabolic processes.", "TORONTO EQUATION:The Toronto equation is a formula suggested for calculating energy requirements in burned patients, based on regression analysis from indirect calorimetry studies.", "HARRIS-BENEDICT FORMULA:The Harris-Benedict formula is a method used to estimate an individual's basal metabolic rate and daily energy requirements.", "BURNED PATIENT:A burned patient is an individual who has sustained burns and may require specialized nutritional support and medical care.", "ESPEN 2013:", "EDMAS GENERALIZADOS:Generalized edema is a common condition in burn patients due to massive fluid infusion, affecting various body systems.", "ÍLEO PARALÍTICO:Íleo paralítico es una condición que afecta la función gastrointestinal, caracterizada por la falta de movimiento en los intestinos. Esta condición puede desarrollarse en pacientes con quemaduras y, en algunos casos, puede permitir el uso de nutrición enteral a bajo flujo. El íleo paralítico a menudo se asocia con una sobrecarga de fluidos, lo que contribuye a la disminución de la motilidad intestinal.", "FÓRMULA DE PARKLAND:The Parkland formula is a method used to calculate the initial fluid resuscitation volume for burn patients based on their weight and burn surface area.", "GUÍAS DE SOCIEDADES INTERNACIONALES:International societies provide guidelines for the management and treatment of critically ill patients, including those with burns.", "REDOXS:The REDOXS study investigated the effects of glutamine (Gln) supplementation in critically ill patients, comparing enteral and parenteral administration methods."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}